{:namespace "app.execution"
 :description "Fn-execution endpoints: POST /api/execute (submit a run), GET /api/execute/:id (poll status), POST /api/execute/:id/cancel (soft cancel). The parse/validate/apply stages delegate to graphden.crud.fn-execution; the apply stage submits the user's fn-graph to a future and either returns the inline result (when it finishes within `:timeout-ms`) or persists a :fn-execution row + returns `{:status :pending :execution-id}` for the client to poll."
 :fns
 [;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Output shapes
  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

  {:name :_execute-parsed-shape
   :description "Output of `:_execute-parsed` — the request body normalised to `{:fn-id :fn-name :args :timeout-ms :persist?}`."
   :type {:fn-id      :uuid
          :fn-name    :text
          :args       :jsonb
          :timeout-ms :int
          :persist?   :bool}}

  {:name :_execute-rejection-shape
   :description "Output of `:_execute-validation` — `{:ok false :error :error-data}` rejection or nil."
   :type {:ok :bool :error :text :error-data :jsonb}}

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Base-fns — stage shims
  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

  ;; --- Stage 1 (Variant-2 decomposition) ---
  ;; Was `(defbase _execute-parsed [request] (parse-execute-request
  ;; request))` delegating to a Clojure helper that read the JSON
  ;; body, parse-uuid'd `:fn-id`, str'd `:fn-name`, `or`'d `:args` to
  ;; `{}`, `or`'d `:timeout-ms` to 10000, and `true?`'d `:persist?`.
  ;;
  ;; Now expressed as graph composition over per-field atoms:
  ;;   parse body → 5 field lookups → 5 per-field transforms → :zipmap
  ;;
  ;; Admins extending the request shape (`:branch-ref`, `:cron-spec`,
  ;; …) edit `:_execute-parsed`'s `:keys`/`:vals` lists instead of a
  ;; Clojure helper.

  {:name :_execute-body
   :description "POST /api/execute request body parsed as JSON."
   :parent :parse-json-body}

  {:name :_execute-body-fn-id
   :parent :get :args {:coll :_execute-body :key {:value :fn-id} :default nil}}

  {:name :_execute-body-fn-name
   :parent :get :args {:coll :_execute-body :key {:value :fn-name} :default nil}}

  {:name :_execute-body-args
   :parent :get :args {:coll :_execute-body :key {:value :args} :default nil}}

  {:name :_execute-body-timeout-ms
   :parent :get :args {:coll :_execute-body :key {:value :timeout-ms} :default nil}}

  {:name :_execute-body-persist
   :parent :get :args {:coll :_execute-body :key {:value :persist?} :default nil}}

  ;; Field-level transforms.

  {:name :_execute-fn-id
   :description "`:fn-id` parsed UUID; nil for blank / non-string / malformed input. Mirrors `parse-uuid-or-clear`'s defensive collapse — every failure path returns nil so downstream guards can produce a clean error response."
   :parent :parse-uuid
   :args {:s :_execute-body-fn-id}}

  {:name :_execute-fn-name
   :description "`:fn-name` coerced to string when present, nil when absent. Nil-safe `(some-> body :fn-name str)` pattern."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test {:parent :some? :args {:value :_execute-body-fn-name}}
          :then {:parent :to-str :args {:value :_execute-body-fn-name}}
          :else nil}}

  {:name :_execute-args
   :description "`:args` map, defaulting to `{}` when absent."
   :parent :coalesce
   :args {:value :_execute-body-args :default {}}}

  {:name :_execute-timeout-ms
   :description "`:timeout-ms`, defaulting to 10000ms when the request didn't override. Matches `default-timeout-ms` in `graphden.crud.fn-execution`."
   :parent :coalesce
   :args {:value :_execute-body-timeout-ms :default 10000}}

  {:name :_execute-persist
   :description "`:persist?` — `true?` semantics. Any truthy-but-not-true value (string, number) → false. The strict equality guards against client-side libraries sending `\"true\"` or `1`."
   :parent :equal?
   :args {:a :_execute-body-persist :b {:value true}}}

  {:name :_execute-parsed
   :description "Stage 1 — JSON body → `{:fn-id :fn-name :args :timeout-ms :persist?}`. Per-field graph composition over `:parse-json-body` + `:get` + (`:parse-uuid` / `:coalesce` / nil-safe `:to-str` / `:equal? true`) — admin-extensible without touching Clojure."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :fn-id}
                 {:value :fn-name}
                 {:value :args}
                 {:value :timeout-ms}
                 {:value :persist?}]
          :vals [:_execute-fn-id
                 :_execute-fn-name
                 :_execute-args
                 :_execute-timeout-ms
                 :_execute-persist]}}

  ;; --- C23: stage 2 of execute as a graph `:cond`. Was a single
  ;; `defbase` delegating to `fn-exec/validate-execute`; now each
  ;; guard is its own `:_execute-*-err` rejection-builder defbase
  ;; (the `defn`s in `graphden.crud.fn-execution`), with a thin
  ;; `:_execute-*-rej?` predicate built as `:parent :some?` over
  ;; the same err. `:cond` is lazy + shares `parsed`, so each
  ;; guard's underlying `parsed` lookup runs at most once per call
  ;; even though predicate + clause-result both read it (call-cache
  ;; dedupes the per-guard `defn` call). The Clojure-level
  ;; composition in `validate-execute` mirrors this order. ---
  ;; --- :_execute-no-fn-err (Variant-2). Was defbase delegating to
  ;; `execute-no-fn-rej` which returned `(when (and (nil? fn-id) (nil?
  ;; fn-name)) {...})`. Now an `:if` over `:and :nil? :nil?` returning
  ;; a `:const` rejection or nil. The rejection body is admin-tunable
  ;; via the `:const`'s `:value`.
  {:name :_execute-no-fn?
   :description "True iff parsed request has neither `:fn-id` nor `:fn-name`."
   :parent :and
   :args {:values [{:parent :nil?
                    :args {:value {:parent :get
                                   :args {:coll {:as :parsed}
                                          :key {:value :fn-id}
                                          :default nil}}}}
                   {:parent :nil?
                    :args {:value {:parent :get
                                   :args {:coll {:as :parsed}
                                          :key {:value :fn-name}
                                          :default nil}}}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-no-fn-rej
   :description "Static rejection envelope for the `:no-fn` guard."
   :parent :const
   :args {:value {:ok false
                  :status :rejected
                  :error "Request must carry :fn-id or :fn-name"
                  :error-data {:reason :no-fn}}}}

  {:name :_execute-no-fn-err
   :description "Static rejection — request carried neither `:fn-id` nor `:fn-name`. Returns the rejection map when the guard fires, else nil. `:if` is lazy, so the rejection literal is only realised when actually needed."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test :_execute-no-fn?
          :then :_execute-no-fn-rej
          :else nil}}

  ;; --- :resolve-fn — atomic primitive. Resolves `{:fn-id ? :fn-name ?}`
  ;; to the full `:fn` row in one storage round-trip; nil when neither
  ;; identifier matches. Wraps `lookup/resolve-fn` (which itself absorbs
  ;; the storage's text-vs-keyword codec ambiguity for `fn.name`).
  ;; Reusable across the validation guards: fn-not-found / running-as-
  ;; svc / unknown-arg all share the same fn-row, so result-cache
  ;; dedupes the DB hit within a single /api/execute call.
  {:name :resolve-fn
   :description "Resolve `parsed.{:fn-id, :fn-name}` to a full `:fn` row; nil when neither identifier resolves."
   :args {:parsed {:type :_execute-parsed-shape}}
   :return-type :jsonb
   :effects #{:db}}

  ;; --- :_execute-fn-not-found-err (Variant-2). Was defbase delegating
  ;; to `execute-fn-not-found-rej`. Now graph-composed: resolve-fn +
  ;; has-anchor? (request carried fn-id or fn-name) + nil-id? +
  ;; dynamic `:str` message. Same lazy `:if` envelope.
  {:name :_execute-fn-row
   :description "Full :fn row for the parsed request, or nil when neither :fn-id nor :fn-name resolves."
   :parent :resolve-fn
   :args {:parsed {:as :parsed}}}

  {:name :_execute-fn-row-id
   :parent :get
   :args {:coll :_execute-fn-row :key {:value :id} :default nil}}

  {:name :_execute-parsed-fn-id
   :parent :get
   :args {:coll {:as :parsed} :key {:value :fn-id} :default nil}}

  {:name :_execute-parsed-fn-name
   :parent :get
   :args {:coll {:as :parsed} :key {:value :fn-name} :default nil}}

  {:name :_execute-has-anchor?
   :description "Request carried `:fn-id` OR `:fn-name`. Distinguishes the no-anchor case (caught by `:_execute-no-fn?`) from the unresolvable-anchor case (caught here)."
   :parent :or
   :args {:values [{:parent :some? :args {:value :_execute-parsed-fn-id}}
                   {:parent :some? :args {:value :_execute-parsed-fn-name}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-fn-not-found?
   :description "True iff request carried an anchor BUT it didn't resolve."
   :parent :and
   :args {:values [:_execute-has-anchor?
                   {:parent :nil? :args {:value :_execute-fn-row-id}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-fn-not-found-anchor
   :description "The anchor cited in the error message — fn-name when supplied, else stringified fn-id. `:coalesce` requires `:value` and `:default` to share a type-var binding, so the UUID fn-id is `:to-str`'d before being passed — the consumer (`:str :parts`) just concatenates regardless."
   :parent :coalesce
   :args {:value :_execute-parsed-fn-name
          :default {:parent :to-str :args {:value :_execute-parsed-fn-id}}}}

  {:name :_execute-fn-not-found-msg
   :parent :str
   :args {:parts [{:value "Function not found: "}
                  :_execute-fn-not-found-anchor]}}

  {:name :_execute-fn-not-found-rej
   :description "Rejection envelope for the fn-not-found guard. Dynamic — `:error` cites the unresolved anchor."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :ok} {:value :status} {:value :error} {:value :error-data}]
          :vals [{:value false}
                 {:value :rejected}
                 :_execute-fn-not-found-msg
                 {:value {:reason :fn-not-found}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-fn-not-found-err
   :description "Dynamic rejection — `:fn-id` / `:fn-name` was supplied but doesn't resolve to a real `:fn` row. Returns the rejection map when the guard fires, else nil. `:resolve-fn` runs once per request thanks to result-cache; downstream guards (`running-as-svc`, `unknown-arg`) share the same `:_execute-fn-row`."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test :_execute-fn-not-found?
          :then :_execute-fn-not-found-rej
          :else nil}}

  ;; --- :_execute-timeout-bad-err (Variant-2). Was defbase delegating
  ;; to `execute-timeout-out-of-range-rej` which returned `(when (or
  ;; (< t 1) (> t 60000)) {...})`. Now graph-composed: parsed-timeout
  ;; lookup + range predicate (`:or :lt :gt`) + `:if` over a `:zipmap`
  ;; envelope. The `:error-data` map cites the bad timeout value.
  {:name :_execute-parsed-timeout
   :description "`:timeout-ms` field of `:_execute-parsed`."
   :parent :get
   :args {:coll {:as :parsed}
          :key {:value :timeout-ms}
          :default nil}}

  {:name :_execute-timeout-bad?
   :description "True iff parsed `:timeout-ms` is < 1 or > 60000 (max-timeout-ms in `graphden.crud.fn-execution`)."
   :parent :or
   :args {:values [{:parent :lt :args {:nums [:_execute-parsed-timeout {:value 1}]}}
                   {:parent :gt :args {:nums [:_execute-parsed-timeout {:value 60000}]}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-timeout-bad-rej-data
   :description "`:error-data` block for the timeout-out-of-range rejection — `{:reason :timeout-out-of-range :timeout-ms <bad-value>}`."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :reason} {:value :timeout-ms}]
          :vals [{:value :timeout-out-of-range}
                 :_execute-parsed-timeout]}}

  {:name :_execute-timeout-bad-rej
   :description "Rejection envelope for the timeout-out-of-range guard. Dynamic — `:error-data` cites the bad value."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :ok} {:value :status} {:value :error} {:value :error-data}]
          :vals [{:value false}
                 {:value :rejected}
                 {:value ":timeout-ms must be in [1, 60000]"}
                 :_execute-timeout-bad-rej-data]}}

  {:name :_execute-timeout-bad-err
   :description "Dynamic rejection — `:timeout-ms` outside `[1, max-timeout-ms]`. Returns the rejection map when out of range, else nil. `:if` is lazy, so the dynamic envelope is built only when the guard fires."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test :_execute-timeout-bad?
          :then :_execute-timeout-bad-rej
          :else nil}}

  ;; --- :_execute-args-too-large-err (Variant-2). Was defbase wrapping
  ;; `(when (> (count (json/generate-string args)) 262144) {...})`.
  ;; Now pure graph composition — no new base-fns needed because
  ;; `:to-json-string` + `:count` already cover the size calc, and
  ;; `:gt` + `:if` + `:zipmap` cover the rest. `max-args-bytes` lives
  ;; in `graphden.crud.fn-execution.persist` (256 KB = 262144); change
  ;; both sides if you tighten / loosen the limit.
  {:name :_execute-args-bytes
   :description "Serialised byte-size of `:args` — `(count (to-json-string args))`. Pure graph composition over existing primitives, no new base-fn."
   :parent :count
   :args {:coll {:parent :to-json-string
                 :args {:data {:parent :get
                               :args {:coll {:as :parsed}
                                      :key {:value :args}
                                      :default nil}}}}}}

  {:name :_execute-args-too-large?
   :description "True iff serialised `:args` exceeds the 256 KB limit."
   :parent :gt
   :args {:nums [:_execute-args-bytes {:value 262144}]}}

  {:name :_execute-args-too-large-rej-data
   :description "`:error-data` block for the args-too-large rejection — `{:reason :args-too-large :bytes <n>}`."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :reason} {:value :bytes}]
          :vals [{:value :args-too-large}
                 :_execute-args-bytes]}}

  {:name :_execute-args-too-large-rej
   :description "Rejection envelope for the args-too-large guard."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :ok} {:value :status} {:value :error} {:value :error-data}]
          :vals [{:value false}
                 {:value :rejected}
                 {:value ":args size exceeds 262144 bytes"}
                 :_execute-args-too-large-rej-data]}}

  {:name :_execute-args-too-large-err
   :description "Dynamic rejection — serialised `:args` payload exceeds 256 KB (`max-args-bytes` in `fn-execution.persist`). Returns the rejection map when the guard fires, else nil."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test :_execute-args-too-large?
          :then :_execute-args-too-large-rej
          :else nil}}

  ;; --- :_execute-running-as-svc-err (Variant-2). Was defbase wrapping
  ;; `(when fn-id (when-let [svc (first (query :service {:fn-id fn-id
  ;; :enabled? true}))] {...}))`. Now graph composition — reuses
  ;; `:_execute-fn-row-id` from `:_execute-fn-not-found-err`, builds the
  ;; query :where via `:zipmap`, takes :first, gates the rejection on
  ;; both fn-id-present AND conflict-present.
  ;; Block 1 Step 2 migration: enabled-services-for-fn check now reads
  ;; :service directly via `:pg-query` + per-row `:decode-row`.
  ;; `:service` is non-versioned, so the version-resolution bypass is
  ;; correct. The where filter combines `:fn-id` + `:enabled?` in an
  ;; `[:and …]` HoneySQL clause; the `fn-id` is per-request so the
  ;; where vector is built via `:vec`.
  {:name :_execute-svc-conflicts
   :description "Enabled `:service` rows whose `:fn-id` matches the resolved fn."
   :parent :map
   :args {:func :_execute-svc-conflicts-decode-row
          :coll :_execute-svc-conflicts-raw}}

  {:name :_execute-svc-conflicts-raw
   :parent :storage-query-call
   :args {:arg :_execute-svc-conflicts-hsql}}

  {:name :_execute-svc-conflicts-hsql
   :parent :assoc
   :args {:map {:value {:select [:*] :from :service}}
          :key {:value :where}
          :value :_execute-svc-conflicts-hsql-where}}

  {:name :_execute-svc-conflicts-hsql-where
   :parent :vec
   :args {:coll [{:value :and}
                 :_execute-svc-conflicts-hsql-fn-id-eq
                 {:value [:= :enabled? true]}]}}

  {:name :_execute-svc-conflicts-hsql-fn-id-eq
   :parent :vec
   :args {:coll [{:value :=} {:value :fn-id} :_execute-fn-row-id]}}

  {:name :_execute-svc-conflicts-decode-row
   :parent :decode-row
   :args {:row {:as :item} :entity-type {:value "service"}}}

  {:name :_execute-conflict-svc
   :description "First conflicting service row (nil when none)."
   :parent :first
   :args {:coll :_execute-svc-conflicts}}

  {:name :_execute-running-as-svc?
   :description "Guard — request resolved to a fn-id AND that fn-id has an enabled service. Lazy `:and` short-circuits when fn-id is nil (caught by `:_execute-no-fn?` / `:_execute-fn-not-found?` earlier in the cond)."
   :parent :and
   :args {:values [{:parent :some? :args {:value :_execute-fn-row-id}}
                   {:parent :some? :args {:value :_execute-conflict-svc}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-running-as-svc-rej-data
   :description "`:error-data` block — cites the conflicting service's id."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :reason} {:value :source} {:value :service-id}]
          :vals [{:value :already-running-as-service}
                 {:value :service}
                 {:parent :get :args {:coll :_execute-conflict-svc :key {:value :id} :default nil}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-running-as-svc-rej
   :description "Rejection envelope for the already-running-as-service guard."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :ok} {:value :status} {:value :error} {:value :error-data}]
          :vals [{:value false}
                 {:value :rejected}
                 {:value "Function is already running as a managed service. Disable the service in /api/entities/service or restart it via /api/services/reconcile."}
                 :_execute-running-as-svc-rej-data]}}

  {:name :_execute-running-as-svc-err
   :description "Dynamic rejection — fn is already alive as a managed `:service` row (the reconciler owns it). Returns the rejection map when both fn-id resolves AND an enabled service references it, else nil."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test :_execute-running-as-svc?
          :then :_execute-running-as-svc-rej
          :else nil}}

  ;; `:free-arg-slot-map` is now declared in `web/crud/fns.edn` so
  ;; both CRUD-write-time guards and this execute-validation guard
  ;; can reference it. Pulled transitively via the `app → web`
  ;; package edge.

  ;; --- :_execute-unknown-arg-err (Variant-2). Was defbase wrapping
  ;; `(let [free-args (lookup/free-arg-slot-map ctx fn-id) unknown
  ;; (remove (set (keys free-args)) (map keyword (keys (:args
  ;; parsed))))] (when (seq unknown) {...}))`. Now graph composition:
  ;; resolve fn-row → look up its free-arg slot map → set-diff against
  ;; supplied arg names → if any leftover, build rejection citing
  ;; both unknown + known. The `lookup/free-arg-slot-map` BFS stays
  ;; inside the new `:free-arg-slot-map` primitive (§3.3 inside the
  ;; atomic boundary).
  {:name :_execute-free-args
   :description "`{arg-name → slot-id}` of the resolved fn's free args. `{}` when fn-row didn't resolve."
   :parent :free-arg-slot-map
   :args {:fn-id :_execute-fn-row-id}}

  {:name :_execute-free-arg-keys
   :description "Vector of free-arg keyword names — the set of acceptable arg keys."
   :parent :keys
   :args {:map :_execute-free-args}}

  ;; Per-iteration predicate — `:item` is a key from `:args parsed`;
  ;; `:_execute-free-arg-keys` is captured from outer scope.
  {:name :_execute-arg-is-unknown?
   :description "True iff the per-iteration arg-key is NOT among the fn's free-arg names. Uses `:position-in` + `:nil?` for membership; converting `:_execute-free-arg-keys` to a set would be cheaper for large fn-defs but typical free-arg counts (1–10) make the linear scan a non-issue."
   :parent :nil?
   :args {:value {:parent :position-in
                  :args {:coll :_execute-free-arg-keys
                         :value {:as :item}}}}}

  {:name :_execute-unknown-args
   :description "Subset of `:args` keys not in the fn's free-arg names."
   :parent :filter
   :args {:pred :_execute-arg-is-unknown?
          :coll :_execute-args-keys}}

  {:name :_execute-unknown-arg?
   :description "Guard fires when fn-id resolved AND at least one supplied arg-key is not a known free-arg. Lazy `:and` short-circuits when fn-id is nil."
   :parent :and
   :args {:values [{:parent :some? :args {:value :_execute-fn-row-id}}
                   {:parent :not :args {:value {:parent :empty? :args {:coll :_execute-unknown-args}}}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-unknown-args-vec
   :description "Vec-coerced version of the filter result for the wire envelope."
   :parent :vec
   :args {:coll :_execute-unknown-args}}

  {:name :_execute-unknown-arg-msg
   :description "`Unknown arg(s): [:foo :bar]` — concatenates the prefix with the unknown vector's `(str)` form."
   :parent :str
   :args {:parts [{:value "Unknown arg(s): "}
                  :_execute-unknown-args-vec]}}

  {:name :_execute-unknown-arg-rej-data
   :description "`:error-data` block — cites both `:unknown` (the offending args) and `:known` (the full free-arg list) so the editor can render a 'did you mean' picker."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :reason} {:value :unknown} {:value :known}]
          :vals [{:value :unknown-arg}
                 :_execute-unknown-args-vec
                 {:parent :vec :args {:coll :_execute-free-arg-keys}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-unknown-arg-rej
   :description "Rejection envelope for the unknown-arg guard."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :ok} {:value :status} {:value :error} {:value :error-data}]
          :vals [{:value false}
                 {:value :rejected}
                 :_execute-unknown-arg-msg
                 :_execute-unknown-arg-rej-data]}}

  {:name :_execute-unknown-arg-err
   :description "Dynamic rejection — one or more `:args` keys aren't valid free-arg names on the target fn. Returns the rejection map when the guard fires, else nil."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test :_execute-unknown-arg?
          :then :_execute-unknown-arg-rej
          :else nil}}

  ;; --- :_execute-malformed-ref-err (Variant-2). Was defbase wrapping
  ;; `(keep (fn [[k v]] (when (and (ref-arg? v) (nil? (parse-ref-fn-id
  ;; v))) {:arg (name k) :raw-ref (:ref v)})) (:args parsed))`. Now
  ;; pure graph composition — no new base-fns. Iterates over `:args`
  ;; keys, filters keys whose value is a `{:ref str}` shape with a
  ;; bad UUID, maps each kept key to the wire shape.
  ;;
  ;; The `:ref-arg?` and `:parse-ref-fn-id` Clojure helpers in
  ;; `crud.fn-execution.persist` remain because `apply-execute` calls
  ;; them through a different code path; eventual decomposition of
  ;; `:_execute-apply` would eliminate the duplication.
  {:name :_execute-parsed-args
   :parent :get
   :args {:coll {:as :parsed} :key {:value :args} :default nil}}

  ;; Per-iteration helpers — `{:as :item}` is the current key into
  ;; `:_execute-parsed-args`, supplied by the HOF.
  {:name :_execute-arg-value
   :description "Value at `args[item]`. `:item` is the HOF iteration variable."
   :parent :get
   :args {:coll :_execute-parsed-args :key {:as :item} :default nil}}

  {:name :_execute-arg-ref
   :description "`(get arg-value :ref)` — the raw ref under the `:ref` key (or nil)."
   :parent :get
   :args {:coll :_execute-arg-value :key {:value :ref} :default nil}}

  {:name :_execute-arg-is-ref-shape?
   :description "True iff `args[item]` is a `{:ref ...}` map."
   :parent :and
   :args {:values [{:parent :is-a? :args {:value :_execute-arg-value :type {:value :map}}}
                   {:parent :contains? :args {:coll :_execute-arg-value :key {:value :ref}}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-arg-ref-bad?
   :description "True iff the ref under `args[item]` doesn't parse as a UUID."
   :parent :nil?
   :args {:value {:parent :parse-uuid :args {:s :_execute-arg-ref}}}}

  {:name :_execute-arg-is-malformed-ref?
   :description "Pure per-key predicate for `:filter` — ref-shape AND ref doesn't parse."
   :parent :and
   :args {:values [:_execute-arg-is-ref-shape? :_execute-arg-ref-bad?]}}

  {:name :_execute-args-keys
   :description "Keys of the parsed `:args` map. `(keys {})` returns nil; `:filter`/`:map` (now eager) treat nil-coll as empty seq per Clojure semantics, so downstream HOFs handle it cleanly without an explicit clamp."
   :parent :keys
   :args {:map :_execute-parsed-args}}

  {:name :_execute-malformed-keys
   :description "Subset of arg-keys whose value is a malformed ref."
   :parent :filter
   :args {:pred :_execute-arg-is-malformed-ref?
          :coll :_execute-args-keys}}

  ;; Per malformed key → `{:arg (name k) :raw-ref (get-in args [k :ref])}`.
  {:name :_execute-malformed-shape
   :description "HOF callback over a malformed key — `{:arg (name k) :raw-ref (get args[k] :ref)}`. `:item` is the key; `:_execute-arg-ref` reads the ref via the same `:item` capture."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :arg} {:value :raw-ref}]
          :vals [{:parent :keyword-to-str :args {:keyword {:as :item}}}
                 :_execute-arg-ref]}}

  {:name :_execute-malformed
   :parent :map
   :args {:func :_execute-malformed-shape
          :coll :_execute-malformed-keys}}

  {:name :_execute-any-malformed?
   :description "Guard — at least one arg is a malformed ref."
   :parent :not
   :args {:value {:parent :empty? :args {:coll :_execute-malformed}}}}

  ;; Error builder fragments.
  {:name :_execute-malformed-arg-names
   :description "List of just the `:arg` names for the error message — `(mapv :arg malformed)`."
   :parent :map
   :args {:func :_execute-malformed-name-of
          :coll :_execute-malformed}}

  {:name :_execute-malformed-name-of
   :parent :get
   :args {:coll {:as :item} :key {:value :arg} :default nil}}

  {:name :_execute-malformed-msg
   :description "`Malformed ref(s) in args: [\"a\" \"b\"]` — concatenates the prefix with the vector's print form."
   :parent :str
   :args {:parts [{:value "Malformed ref(s) in args: "}
                  {:parent :vec :args {:coll :_execute-malformed-arg-names}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-malformed-ref-rej-data
   :description "`:error-data` block — cites every malformed `{:arg :raw-ref}` entry."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :reason} {:value :malformed}]
          :vals [{:value :malformed-ref}
                 {:parent :vec :args {:coll :_execute-malformed}}]}}

  {:name :_execute-malformed-ref-rej
   :description "Rejection envelope for the malformed-ref guard."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :ok} {:value :status} {:value :error} {:value :error-data}]
          :vals [{:value false}
                 {:value :rejected}
                 :_execute-malformed-msg
                 :_execute-malformed-ref-rej-data]}}

  {:name :_execute-malformed-ref-err
   :description "Dynamic rejection — one or more `{:ref str}` args carry an unparseable UUID. Returns the rejection map when any malformed ref exists, else nil. Lazy `:if` — the rejection envelope only builds when the guard fires."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test :_execute-any-malformed?
          :then :_execute-malformed-ref-rej
          :else nil}}

  {:name :_execute-no-fn-rej?
   :description "Predicate — `:_execute-no-fn-err` produced a rejection."
   :parent :some?
   :args {:value :_execute-no-fn-err}}

  {:name :_execute-fn-not-found-rej?
   :description "Predicate — `:_execute-fn-not-found-err` produced a rejection."
   :parent :some?
   :args {:value :_execute-fn-not-found-err}}

  {:name :_execute-timeout-bad-rej?
   :description "Predicate — `:_execute-timeout-bad-err` produced a rejection."
   :parent :some?
   :args {:value :_execute-timeout-bad-err}}

  {:name :_execute-args-too-large-rej?
   :description "Predicate — `:_execute-args-too-large-err` produced a rejection."
   :parent :some?
   :args {:value :_execute-args-too-large-err}}

  {:name :_execute-running-as-svc-rej?
   :description "Predicate — `:_execute-running-as-svc-err` produced a rejection."
   :parent :some?
   :args {:value :_execute-running-as-svc-err}}

  {:name :_execute-unknown-arg-rej?
   :description "Predicate — `:_execute-unknown-arg-err` produced a rejection."
   :parent :some?
   :args {:value :_execute-unknown-arg-err}}

  {:name :_execute-malformed-ref-rej?
   :description "Predicate — `:_execute-malformed-ref-err` produced a rejection."
   :parent :some?
   :args {:value :_execute-malformed-ref-err}}

  {:name :_execute-validation
   :description "Stage 2 — guards wired as a flat `:cond` over per-guard rejection-builders. Returns the first matching `{:ok false :status :rejected :error :error-data}` rejection, or `nil` when the request is well-formed (the `{:value true}` clause). `parsed` is bound once at this `:cond` so every guard shares it. Order mirrors the Clojure `validate-execute` composition."
   :parent :cond
   :args {:clauses [:_execute-no-fn-rej?            :_execute-no-fn-err
                    :_execute-fn-not-found-rej?     :_execute-fn-not-found-err
                    :_execute-timeout-bad-rej?      :_execute-timeout-bad-err
                    :_execute-args-too-large-rej?   :_execute-args-too-large-err
                    :_execute-running-as-svc-rej?   :_execute-running-as-svc-err
                    :_execute-unknown-arg-rej?      :_execute-unknown-arg-err
                    :_execute-malformed-ref-rej?    :_execute-malformed-ref-err
                    {:value true}                   {:value nil}]
          :parsed :_execute-parsed}}

  {:name :_execute-apply
   :description "Stage 3 — submit future, deref with timeout. Returns inline `{:status :succeeded :result …}` for fast pure fns, or `{:status :pending :execution-id}` when timeout/effects/persist? forced async."
   :args {:parsed {:type :_execute-parsed-shape}}
   :return-type :jsonb
   :effects #{:db}}

  {:name :_execute-rejected?
   :description "True when `:_execute-validation` produced a rejection."
   :parent :some?
   :args {:value {:as :validation}}}

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Composed: parse → (if rejected then rejection else apply)
  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

  {:name :execute
   :description "POST /api/execute pipeline. Parse → validate → on success apply."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test :_execute-rejected?
          :then :_execute-validation
          :else :_execute-apply
          :validation :_execute-validation
          :parsed :_execute-parsed}}

  {:name :execute-handler
   :description "Ring handler for POST /api/execute — wraps `:execute` in a JSON 200 response."
   :parent :json-handler
   :args {:data :execute}}

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; GET /api/execute/:id — read a persisted row
  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

  ;; --- C18 atoms: get-execution + cancel-execution variant-2.
  ;; Shared parse (`_exec-id-parsed`) + shared 404 builder
  ;; (`_exec-err-not-found`).
  {:name :_exec-id-from-path-params
   :description "Try the reitit-enriched `:path-params {:id …}` field first — the canonical place when the request flows through reitit's enrich-request middleware."
   :parent :get-in
   :args {:map {:as :request}
          :path [{:value :path-params} {:value :id}]
          :default nil}}

  {:name :_exec-id-from-uri
   :description "Fallback: parse the id directly from the URL when the handler is invoked with a raw http-kit request (e.g. through `:branch-routing-wrap`) that hasn't gone through reitit's enrich-request — `:path-params` is nil there."
   :parent :uri-segment-after
   :args {:marker {:value "execute"}}}

  {:name :_exec-id-raw
   :description "Raw id string from the URL — `:path-params` when present, else the URI segment after `execute/`."
   :parent :coalesce
   :args {:value :_exec-id-from-path-params
          :default :_exec-id-from-uri}}

  {:name :_exec-id-parsed
   :description "Parse the `:id` path segment for both `/api/execute/:id` and `/api/execute/:id/cancel` into `{:id <uuid|nil> :id-raw <raw>}`. Raw form preserved for the dynamic 404 message."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :id} {:value :id-raw}]
          :vals [{:parent :parse-uuid :args {:s :_exec-id-raw}}
                 :_exec-id-raw]}}

  {:name :_exec-err-not-found
   :description "Dynamic 404 builder — cites the raw id-segment. Shared between get + cancel."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [:ok :error]
          :vals [false
                 {:parent :str
                  :args {:parts ["Execution not found: "
                                 {:parent :get
                                  :args {:coll {:as :parsed}
                                         :key {:value :id-raw}
                                         :default nil}}]}}]}}

  ;; --- :get-execution / :cancel-execution! — atomic DB primitives.
  {:name :get-execution
   :description "Read a :fn-execution row by id (joined with arg + arg-item rows). nil when id is nil or doesn't resolve."
   :args {:id {:type [:union :null :uuid] :description "Target execution id."}}
   :return-type :jsonb
   :effects #{:db}}

  {:name :cancel-execution!
   :description "Soft-cancel an in-flight execution by id. nil when id is nil or doesn't resolve. Side effects: `:db` (sets `:cancel-requested?` on the persisted row) + `:process` (interrupts the in-process future via `future-cancel`)."
   :args {:id {:type [:union :null :uuid] :description "Target execution id."}}
   :return-type :jsonb
   :effects #{:db :process}}

  {:name :_get-exec-loaded
   :description "Load the persisted execution row by id; nil when not found. Reuses the `:get-execution` primitive."
   :parent :get-execution
   :args {:id {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :parsed} :key {:value :id} :default nil}}}}

  {:name :_get-exec-missing?
   :description "Guard — load returned nil."
   :parent :nil?
   :args {:value {:as :loaded}}}

  {:name :_get-exec-apply
   :description "Success branch — return the loaded row verbatim. `:const` over the captured `:loaded` cond shared-arg."
   :parent :const
   :args {:value {:as :loaded}}}

  {:name :_get-execution
   :description "GET /api/execute/:id — flat `:cond` of `[exec-missing? → 404-dynamic, true → return-loaded]`. `parsed` + `loaded` are shared sub-results bound once at the `:cond` fn-def."
   :parent :cond
   :args {:clauses [:_get-exec-missing? :_exec-err-not-found
                    {:value true}        :_get-exec-apply]
          :parsed :_exec-id-parsed
          :loaded :_get-exec-loaded}}

  {:name :get-execution-handler
   :description "Ring handler for GET /api/execute/:id."
   :parent :json-handler
   :args {:data :_get-execution}}

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; POST /api/execute/:id/cancel — soft cancel an in-flight execution
  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

  {:name :_cancel-exec-applied
   :description "Call `:cancel-execution!` — no-op (returns nil) when id is invalid OR no row matches. Otherwise sets `:cancel-requested?` on the row + `future-cancel`s the in-process handle."
   :parent :cancel-execution!
   :args {:id {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :parsed} :key {:value :id} :default nil}}}}

  {:name :_cancel-exec-missing?
   :description "Guard — cancel returned nil (bad id OR no row)."
   :parent :nil?
   :args {:value {:as :applied}}}

  {:name :_cancel-exec-apply
   :description "Success branch — return the cancel-result-map verbatim. `:const` over the captured `:applied` cond shared-arg."
   :parent :const
   :args {:value {:as :applied}}}

  {:name :_cancel-execution
   :description "POST /api/execute/:id/cancel — flat `:cond` of `[cancel-missing? → 404-dynamic, true → return-applied]`. Reuses C18's `_exec-id-parsed` parser + `_exec-err-not-found` builder."
   :parent :cond
   :args {:clauses [:_cancel-exec-missing? :_exec-err-not-found
                    {:value true}           :_cancel-exec-apply]
          :parsed :_exec-id-parsed
          :applied :_cancel-exec-applied}}

  {:name :cancel-execution-handler
   :description "Ring handler for POST /api/execute/:id/cancel."
   :parent :json-handler
   :args {:data :_cancel-execution}}

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; GET /api/executions?fn-id=X — list recent runs for one fn
  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

  ;; --- C6 atoms: parse / no-anchor? / by-version? / two apply paths.
  ;; --- :_list-exec-parsed (Variant-2). Was defbase delegating to
  ;; `parse-list-executions-request`. Now graph composition:
  ;;   - `:query-param` (new web/crud primitive) for each of the 3
  ;;     query params,
  ;;   - `:parse-uuid` for `:fn-id` and `:version-id` (returns nil on
  ;;     blank / non-string / malformed — matches the legacy
  ;;     `parse-uuid-or-clear` semantics),
  ;;   - `:try`-wrapped `:parse-int` for `:limit` so a non-numeric
  ;;     `?limit=abc` collapses to nil instead of 500-ing.

  {:name :_list-exec-fn-id-raw
   :parent :query-param
   :args {:request {:as :request} :param-name {:value "fn-id"}}}

  {:name :_list-exec-version-id-raw
   :parent :query-param
   :args {:request {:as :request} :param-name {:value "fn-version-id"}}}

  {:name :_list-exec-limit-raw
   :parent :query-param
   :args {:request {:as :request} :param-name {:value "limit"}}}

  {:name :_list-exec-limit-parsed-body
   :description "`:try` body — :parse-int throws NumberFormatException on non-numeric input. The wrapping :try collapses that to nil. The `:type :text` override on the ref-binding tells the type-checker that `:_list-exec-limit-raw`'s nullable surface is excluded under the `:_list-exec-limit` `:if`-guard (`(:some? :_list-exec-limit-raw)` clears it)."
   :parent :parse-int
   :args {:s {:ref :_list-exec-limit-raw :type :text}}}

  {:name :_list-exec-limit
   :description "Parsed `?limit` integer; nil when absent / non-numeric."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test {:parent :some? :args {:value :_list-exec-limit-raw}}
          :then {:parent :try
                 :args {:body :_list-exec-limit-parsed-body
                        ;; `:on-throw` is a `[:fn :any]` callable invoked WITH
                        ;; the caught exception. A literal `{:value nil}` would
                        ;; type-fail against the slot's callable shape; wrap in
                        ;; `:const` so the binding is a (fn [_] nil) shape.
                        :on-throw {:parent :const :args {:value nil}}}}
          :else nil}}

  {:name :_list-exec-parsed
   :description "Parse `GET /api/executions` query-string into `{:fn-id :version-id :limit}`."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :fn-id} {:value :version-id} {:value :limit}]
          :vals [{:parent :parse-uuid :args {:s :_list-exec-fn-id-raw}}
                 {:parent :parse-uuid :args {:s :_list-exec-version-id-raw}}
                 :_list-exec-limit]}}

  {:name :_list-exec-no-anchor?
   :description "Guard — neither `?fn-id` nor `?fn-version-id` was supplied. `:and [nil? (get parsed :version-id)] [nil? (get parsed :fn-id)]`."
   :parent :and
   :args {:values [{:parent :nil? :args {:value {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :parsed} :key {:value :version-id} :default nil}}}}
                   {:parent :nil? :args {:value {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :parsed} :key {:value :fn-id} :default nil}}}}]}}

  {:name :_list-exec-by-version?
   :description "Dispatch — `?fn-version-id` supplied. Wins over `?fn-id` when both are present."
   :parent :some?
   :args {:value {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :parsed} :key {:value :version-id} :default nil}}}}

  ;; Phase 4 follow-up — list-executions-by-version decomposition.
  ;; `:fn-execution` is non-versioned (write-once log), so the SQL
  ;; goes through `:pg-query` directly with `WHERE fn-version-id = ?`
  ;; + `ORDER BY started-at DESC` + `LIMIT <clamped>`. Per-row
  ;; `:decode-row` for snake_case → kebab-case + timestamp parsing.
  ;; Limit clamp is a `:cond` graph over the same nil/non-number/
  ;; lt-1/gt-max ladder the Clojure helper used.
  {:name :_list-exec-apply-by-version
   :description "Return executions for the specific `:fn-version-id`."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [{:value :ok} {:value :executions}]
          :vals [{:value true} :_list-exec-by-version-rows]}}

  {:name :_list-exec-by-version-rows
   :description "Latest-first slice of the executions for this version, capped at the clamped limit."
   :parent :take
   :args {:count {:ref :_list-exec-clamped-limit :type :non-negative-int}
          :coll :_list-exec-by-version-rows-desc}}

  {:name :_list-exec-by-version-rows-desc
   :parent :reverse
   :args {:coll :_list-exec-by-version-rows-asc}}

  {:name :_list-exec-by-version-rows-asc
   :parent :sort-by
   :args {:key-fn :_list-exec-started-at-key
          :coll :_list-exec-by-version-decoded}}

  {:name :_list-exec-started-at-key
   :parent :get :args {:coll {:as :item} :key {:value :started-at} :default nil}}

  {:name :_list-exec-by-version-decoded
   :parent :map
   :args {:func :_list-exec-decode-row :coll :_list-exec-by-version-raw}}

  {:name :_list-exec-decode-row
   :parent :decode-row
   :args {:row {:as :item} :entity-type {:value "fn-execution"}}}

  {:name :_list-exec-by-version-raw
   :parent :storage-query-call
   :args {:arg :_list-exec-by-version-hsql}}

  {:name :_list-exec-by-version-hsql
   :parent :assoc
   :args {:map {:value {:select [:*] :from :fn-execution}}
          :key {:value :where}
          :value :_list-exec-by-version-hsql-where}}

  {:name :_list-exec-by-version-hsql-where
   :parent :vec
   :args {:coll [{:value :=} {:value :fn-version-id} :_list-exec-version-id]}}

  {:name :_list-exec-version-id
   :parent :get :args {:coll {:as :parsed} :key {:value :version-id} :default nil}}

  ;; Limit clamp. The Clojure version: nil/not-number/<1 → 20,
  ;; > 100 → 100, else (long limit). Express as flat `:cond` over
  ;; the parsed `:limit` field.
  {:name :_list-exec-limit-parsed
   :parent :get :args {:coll {:as :parsed} :key {:value :limit} :default nil}}

  {:name :_list-exec-clamped-limit
   :parent :cond
   :args {:clauses [:_list-exec-limit-invalid?  {:value 20}
                    :_list-exec-limit-over-max? {:value 100}
                    {:value true}                :_list-exec-limit-parsed]}}

  {:name :_list-exec-limit-invalid?
   :parent :or
   :args {:values [{:parent :nil? :args {:value :_list-exec-limit-parsed}}
                   :_list-exec-limit-less-than-1?]}}

  ;; `:_list-exec-limit-parsed` declares `[:union :int :null]` —
  ;; non-null asserted here because the sole consumer
  ;; `:_list-exec-limit-invalid?` short-circuits on `:or` after the
  ;; preceding `(:nil? :_list-exec-limit-parsed)` clause, so this
  ;; branch only evaluates when the parsed limit IS an int.
  {:name :_list-exec-limit-less-than-1?
   :parent :gt
   :args {:nums [{:value 1} {:ref :_list-exec-limit-parsed :type :int}]}}

  {:name :_list-exec-limit-over-max?
   :parent :gt
   :args {:nums [{:ref :_list-exec-limit-parsed :type :int} {:value 100}]}}

  ;; `:_list-exec-apply-by-fn` — same shape as `-by-version`, but the
  ;; version-id is RESOLVED from the logical fn-id via
  ;; `:resolve-fn-version-id` (a thin §3.1 wrap around `fn-execution.
  ;; lookup/resolve-fn-version-id`). The reuses-the-version-path :if
  ;; emits `{:ok true :executions []}` when the fn has no version
  ;; visible on the active branch (e.g. inherited-only, never written).
  {:name :resolve-fn-version-id
   :description "Resolve a logical fn-id to its current-branch version-id. nil when the fn has no version visible on the active branch."
   :args {:fn-id {:type :uuid}}
   :return-type [:union :null :uuid]
   :effects #{:db}}

  {:name :_list-exec-apply-by-fn
   :description "Return executions for the logical `:fn-id` (current branch's resolved version)."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test :_list-exec-by-fn-version-found?
          :then :_list-exec-apply-by-fn-with-version
          :else :_list-exec-apply-by-fn-empty
          :version-id :_list-exec-by-fn-version-id
          ;; Carry the OUTER :parsed's :limit out under a fresh
          ;; name so the synth below doesn't have to re-read the
          ;; OUTER :parsed (which the by-version chain rewrites to
          ;; `:_list-exec-by-fn-synth-parsed` — a self-reference
          ;; that loops the executor at runtime).
          :outer-limit {:parent :get
                        :args {:coll {:as :parsed}
                               :key {:value :limit}
                               :default nil}}}}

  {:name :_list-exec-by-fn-version-id
   :parent :resolve-fn-version-id
   :args {:fn-id {:parent :get :type :uuid
                  :args {:coll {:as :parsed} :key {:value :fn-id} :default nil}}}}

  {:name :_list-exec-by-fn-version-found?
   :parent :some? :args {:value {:as :version-id}}}

  ;; When a version IS resolvable, reuse the by-version path's query
  ;; via a small parsed-shape adapter — synthesize a `{:version-id <resolved>}`
  ;; that the version-path's `:_list-exec-version-id` reader picks up.
  {:name :_list-exec-apply-by-fn-with-version
   :parent :_list-exec-apply-by-version
   :args {:parsed :_list-exec-by-fn-synth-parsed}}

  {:name :_list-exec-by-fn-synth-parsed
   :description "Carry the resolved version-id + the OUTER limit into the by-version graph composition. The limit comes via `:outer-limit` from `:_list-exec-apply-by-fn` (NOT a re-read of `:as :parsed`) — at this level `:parsed` is BEING REDEFINED to point at this same fn, so re-reading it would loop. The structural shape mirrors `:_list-exec-by-version`'s declared `:parsed` (fn-id + version-id + limit) so the by-version's typed downstream chain doesn't fail open-record subtype against the missing `:fn-id`."
   :parent :assoc
   :args {:map {:parent :zipmap
                :args {:keys [{:value :limit} {:value :fn-id}]
                       :vals [{:as :outer-limit :type [:union :int :null]}
                              {:as :fn-id :type [:union :null :uuid]}]}}
          :key {:value :version-id}
          :value {:as :version-id}}}

  {:name :_list-exec-apply-by-fn-empty
   :description "Fn has no version visible on the current branch → empty execution list."
   :parent :const
   :args {:value {:ok true :executions []}}}

  {:name :_list-exec-err-no-anchor
   :parent :const
   :return-type :_error-result-shape
   :args {:value {:ok false :error "missing or invalid ?fn-id / ?fn-version-id query parameter"}}}

  {:name :_list-executions-by-fn
   :description "GET /api/executions — flat `:cond` of `[no-anchor? → err, by-version? → version-apply, true → fn-apply]`. `:cond` is lazy, so each apply runs only on its branch. `parsed` is bound once at the `:cond` fn-def."
   :parent :cond
   :args {:clauses [:_list-exec-no-anchor? :_list-exec-err-no-anchor
                    :_list-exec-by-version? :_list-exec-apply-by-version
                    {:value true}            :_list-exec-apply-by-fn]
          :parsed :_list-exec-parsed}}

  {:name :list-executions-handler
   :description "Ring handler for GET /api/executions."
   :parent :json-handler
   :args {:data :_list-executions-by-fn}}

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; POST /api/services/reconcile — Phase 1 hot-reload trigger
  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

  {:name :_reconcile-services-apply
   :description "Stage 1 — single recon-library call producing the reconcile summary (`{:started [ids] :stopped [ids]}`). Wrapped into `{:ok true :reconcile …}` by the fn-def below."
   :args {:request {:type :ring-request-shape}}
   :return-type :jsonb
   :effects #{:db :io}}

  {:name :_reconcile-services
   :description "Wrap the recon summary into the public response shape. Admins can extend the response by adding keys here without touching impls."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [:ok :reconcile]
          :vals [{:value true}
                 :_reconcile-services-apply]}}

  {:name :reconcile-services-handler
   :description "Ring handler for POST /api/services/reconcile."
   :parent :json-handler
   :args {:data :_reconcile-services}}

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; GET /api/services — list service rows + in-process running state
  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

  ;; --- C17 atoms: list-services linear ETL decomposition.
  ;; Five named stages glued by a non-:cond graph (pure variant-1
  ;; data composition). Each stage's purpose is visible at the
  ;; graph level instead of buried in a 20-line `defbase` body.
  ;;
  ;; --- Block 1 Step 2 migration: Stage 1 now reads :service directly
  ;; via `:pg-query` + `:decode-row` instead of the thin
  ;; `:list-entities` wrap. `:service` is NON-VERSIONED (per
  ;; `versioning.storage.resolution/entity-config`), so a raw
  ;; pg-query bypasses the version-resolution layer correctly. The
  ;; HoneySQL is visible in the graph: admins can add `:where` /
  ;; `:order-by` / joins without going through `:list-entities`'s
  ;; generic interface.
  {:name :_list-services-rows
   :description "Stage 1 — every `:service` row decoded to graphden's canonical shape. Direct `:pg-query` + per-row `:decode-row` composition (Block 1 Step 2 migration off `:list-entities`)."
   :parent :map
   :args {:func :_list-services-decode-row
          :coll :_list-services-rows-raw}}

  {:name :_list-services-rows-raw
   :description "Raw `SELECT * FROM service` — snake_case column keys, undecoded enum/jsonb. Decoded per-row by `:_list-services-decode-row`."
   :parent :storage-query-call
   :args {:arg {:value {:select [:*] :from :service}}}}

  {:name :_list-services-decode-row
   :description "HOF callback — decode one raw `:service` row to graphden's canonical shape."
   :parent :decode-row
   :args {:row {:as :item}
          :entity-type {:value "service"}}}

  ;; Block 1 Step 2 Phase 3: `:fn` is a VERSIONED entity. The
  ;; per-entity-type wrapper `:resolve-fn-rows` pins everything
  ;; entity-specific (version-id-field, version-data-fields, the
  ;; SQL for the version table). Per-site we only wire `:identities`
  ;; (the SELECT against `:fn` identity table) and `:branch-chain`
  ;; (the parent chain to scan).
  {:name :_list-services-fn-rows
   :description "Stage 2a — all `:fn` rows visible on the current branch."
   :parent :resolve-fn-rows
   :args {:identities :_list-services-fn-identities
          :branch-chain :current-branch-chain}}

  {:name :_list-services-fn-identities
   :parent :storage-query-identities
   :args {:entity-type {:value "fn"}
          :where {:value {}}}}

  {:name :_list-services-fn-id-name-pair
   :description "HOF callback per fn-row — emit a `[:id :name]` 2-vec so `:into {}` folds the sequence into a `{id → name}` index."
   :parent :vec
   :args {:coll [{:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :item} :key {:value :id} :default nil}}
                 {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :item} :key {:value :name} :default nil}}]}}

  {:name :_list-services-fn-id-name-pairs
   :description "Stage 2b — map the per-fn `[:id :name]` builder over every fn row."
   :parent :map
   :args {:func :_list-services-fn-id-name-pair
          :coll :_list-services-fn-rows}}

  {:name :_list-services-fn-names
   :description "Stage 2 — build `{fn-id → name}` index over `:fn` rows (one query, constant-time lookups for the join below)."
   :parent :into
   :args {:to {:value {}}
          :from :_list-services-fn-id-name-pairs}}

  ;; --- :running-entry atomic primitive ----------------------------
  ;; Single library call — atom-deref + key lookup on `@recon/running`.
  ;; The downstream reshape lives in `:enrich-running` graph fn-def
  ;; below; admins can add `:thread-id` / `:port` / rename
  ;; `:stopper-set?` without touching Clojure.
  {:name :running-entry
   :description "Raw per-service running-info entry from `@recon/running` — `{:stopper :started-at :start-attempts :start-failed-at}` or nil. Atomic boundary; the JSON shape reshape lives in `:enrich-running`."
   :args {:service-id {:type :any :description "Service's row id."}}
   :return-type :jsonb
   :effects #{}}

  ;; --- :enrich-running graph composition --------------------------
  ;; Per-service reshape into a JSON-safe map. Was a `defbase` that
  ;; hardcoded `{:stopper-set? :started-at :start-attempts
  ;; :start-failed-at}` in Clojure; now pure graph composition over
  ;; `:running-entry` + `:if (some? entry)` + `:zipmap`. Admins can
  ;; vary the response shape by reparenting `:_enrich-running-shape`.

  {:name :_enrich-running-entry
   :description "Closure-capture seed — the raw entry pulled off the reconciler atom. Referenced by the per-field readers below."
   :parent :running-entry
   :args {:service-id {:as :service-id}}}

  {:name :_enrich-running-stopper-set?
   :description "Boolean coercion of `:stopper` (a Clojure callable or nil) for the JSON wire."
   :parent :is-a?
   :args {:value {:parent :get :args {:coll :_enrich-running-entry :key {:value :stopper} :default nil}}
          :type {:value :any}}}

  {:name :_enrich-running-started-at-raw
   :parent :get :args {:coll :_enrich-running-entry :key {:value :started-at} :default nil}}

  {:name :_enrich-running-started-at
   :description "Nil-safe stringify (mirrors `(some-> :started-at str)`)."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test {:parent :some? :args {:value :_enrich-running-started-at-raw}}
          :then {:parent :to-str :args {:value :_enrich-running-started-at-raw}}
          :else {:value nil}}}

  {:name :_enrich-running-start-failed-at-raw
   :parent :get :args {:coll :_enrich-running-entry :key {:value :start-failed-at} :default nil}}

  {:name :_enrich-running-start-failed-at
   :description "Nil-safe stringify (mirrors `(some-> :start-failed-at str)`)."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test {:parent :some? :args {:value :_enrich-running-start-failed-at-raw}}
          :then {:parent :to-str :args {:value :_enrich-running-start-failed-at-raw}}
          :else {:value nil}}}

  {:name :_enrich-running-shape
   :description "4-field JSON shape — `{:stopper-set? :started-at :start-attempts :start-failed-at}`. Admins can reparent / reorder to vary the response shape."
   :parent :zipmap
   :return-type {:stopper-set? :bool
                 :started-at [:union :null :text]
                 :start-attempts :any
                 :start-failed-at [:union :null :text]}
   :args {:keys [{:value :stopper-set?} {:value :started-at} {:value :start-attempts} {:value :start-failed-at}]
          :vals [:_enrich-running-stopper-set?
                 :_enrich-running-started-at
                 {:parent :get :args {:coll :_enrich-running-entry :key {:value :start-attempts} :default nil}}
                 :_enrich-running-start-failed-at]}}

  {:name :enrich-running
   :description "Reshape the in-process running entry for `service-id` (from `@recon/running`) into a JSON-safe map. nil when nothing is registered. Pure graph composition over `:running-entry` + `:if (some?)` + 4-field `:zipmap` reshape."
   :parent :if
   :args {:test {:parent :some? :args {:value :_enrich-running-entry}}
          :then :_enrich-running-shape
          :else {:value nil}
          :service-id {:type :uuid
                       :description "Service entity id — used to look up the running entry off the reconciler's in-process atom."}}}

  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; :_list-services-enriched — graph-composed ETL Stage 3
  ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; Was `(mapv (fn [s] {:id … :fn-id … :fn-name (get names …)
  ;; :running (enrich-running …)}) rows)` — a single defbase that
  ;; hardcoded the per-row reshape. Now an `:map` HOF over `:rows`
  ;; with a `:_list-services-row-shape` callback that uses `:zipmap`
  ;; for the field map. `:names` is captured from the outer scope via
  ;; `{:as :names}` — closure-capture per `docs/CLOSURE_CAPTURE.md`,
  ;; the same pattern `:_merge-in-fn` uses for `:update-in`.

  {:name :_list-services-enriched
   :description "Stage 3 — `:map` each service row to the JSON-safe shape (`:fn-name` joined from the index, `:running` snapshot from the in-process atom). Composition is open: admins can extend the `:keys`/`:vals` lists in `:_list-services-row-shape` without editing Clojure."
   :parent :map
   :args {:func :_list-services-row-shape
          :coll {:as :rows}}}

  {:name :_list-services-row-shape
   :description "Single-row reshape — `(fn [s] {:id (:id s) :fn-id (:fn-id s) :fn-name (get names (:fn-id s)) :enabled? (:enabled? s) :restart-policy (:restart-policy s) :branch-id (:branch-id s) :running (enrich-running (:id s))})`. The HOF callback of `:_list-services-enriched`; `:names` is captured from the outer fn-def's free args. `:branch-id` is nullable (legacy rows pre-date the field); the editor service popover uses it to pre-fill the branch picker."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [:id :fn-id :fn-name :enabled? :restart-policy :branch-id :running]
          :vals [{:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :item} :key {:value :id} :default nil}}
                 {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :item} :key {:value :fn-id} :default nil}}
                 {:parent :get
                  :args {:coll {:as :names}
                         :key {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :item} :key {:value :fn-id} :default nil}}
                         :default nil}}
                 {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :item} :key {:value :enabled?} :default nil}}
                 {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :item} :key {:value :restart-policy} :default nil}}
                 {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :item} :key {:value :branch-id} :default nil}}
                 {:parent :enrich-running
                  :args {:service-id {:parent :get :args {:coll {:as :item} :key {:value :id} :default nil}}}}]}}

  {:name :_list-services
   :description "Stage 4 — wrap enriched-services into the final `{:ok true :services}` response."
   :parent :zipmap
   :args {:keys [:ok :services]
          :vals [{:value true}
                 {:as :enriched}]}}

  {:name :_list-services-data
   :description "GET /api/services — Stage-4 wrap of the enriched-services sub-result. Each stage is named in fns.edn so the editor's graph view surfaces the pipeline."
   :parent :_list-services
   :args {:enriched :_list-services-enriched
          ;; Sub-results fed into the enrichment.
          :rows :_list-services-rows
          :names :_list-services-fn-names}}

  {:name :list-services-handler
   :description "Ring handler for GET /api/services."
   :parent :json-handler
   :args {:data :_list-services-data}}]}
